diff --git a/debian/patches/0902-allow-options-on-derived-hw-devices.patch b/debian/patches/0902-allow-options-on-derived-hw-devices.patch
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/debian/patches/0902-allow-options-on-derived-hw-devices.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,71 @@
+Allow options on a derived hardware device.
+
+-init_hw_device <type>=<name>@<source> cannot carry device options. The '@'
+branch of hw_device_init_from_string() hands the entire remainder of the string
+to hw_device_get_by_name(), so a comma and everything after it become part of
+the source device name:
+
+    -init_hw_device vulkan=vk@dr,disable_multiplane=1
+    Invalid device specification "vulkan=vk@dr,disable_multiplane=1":
+        invalid source device name
+
+That reads like a typo and is not one -- it looked for a device literally named
+"dr,disable_multiplane=1". No separator helps, because the branch never parses
+options at all: it calls av_hwdevice_ctx_create_derived(), which takes no
+AVDictionary. av_hwdevice_ctx_create_derived_opts() does, and the ':' branch a
+few lines above already contains exactly the parsing this needs.
+
+THIS AFFECTS EVERY DEVICE OPTION ON A DERIVED DEVICE, not one in particular.
+Vulkan's disable_multiplane is merely the one that exposed it: it is honoured on
+a derived device -- vulkan_device_derive() forwards opts, and its DRM case
+passes the function argument as 0 so the dictionary value wins -- yet there is
+no command line that can set it.
+
+The source device name and the options are freed at the function's existing
+done: label, so no new cleanup is introduced. Without a comma the behaviour is
+byte-identical to before: options stays NULL and the call is equivalent to the
+one it replaces.
+
+Full rationale, and what this does and does not buy:
+docs/patches/0005-allow-options-on-derived-hw-devices.md in the build-recipe
+repo.
+
